            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for a ploughman, then the Latin for to harrow."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: messis, messis",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "messis, messis — \"the harvest, the crop\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Aestāte messis est, et omnēs in agrīs labōrant. In summer it is harvest time, and everyone works in the fields."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Third declension, feminine: messis, messis f., accusative often messem. From metō 'to reap'."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "messis is built on metō 'to reap, to mow' (EtymDB; Lewis & Short), from PIE h₂meh₁- 'to mow'. English mow and meadow are Germanic cousins."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
